  "nodeId": "boss",
  "role": "boss",
  "title": "Forty-Two Fireflies and One Wrong Jar",
  "academicTarget": {
    "domain": "math",
    "skill": "math.multiplication: synthesis of equal-groups construction, missing-factor reasoning, and word-problem interpretation on unseen totals whose multiple factor pairs are disambiguated only by a stated physical constraint",
    "targets": [
      "math.multiplication.equal_groups",
      "math.multiplication.missing_factor",
      "math.multiplication.word_problem_interpretation"
    ]
  },
  "plannerPrescription": "Build a three-round packing instrument for a 9-year-old. Round 1: 42 fireflies, child sets jar count and fireflies-per-jar by dragging; the cart accepts only arrangements where every jar is equally filled and no firefly is left in the dome. Multiple pairs work (2×21, 3×14, 6×7, 7×6, 14×3, 21×2), so let the child commit freely — then reveal the cart shelf holds exactly 6 jars and re-run their choice against it. Round 2: 56 fireflies, the shelf constraint is stated up front as \"7 jars, all equal\" — the child must reason to the missing per-jar count, and the jars visibly overflow or sit half-lit if wrong, spilling fireflies that fly off and shrink the swarm. Round 3: a written order from the bridgekeeper — \"I need 9 lanterns lit tonight and each lantern eats 4 jars\" — the total is not given; the child must interpret what to compute and pack from a dome of 48, discovering they are short and reporting the shortfall. Escalate the fade: a light meter drains across all three rounds and drains faster after each spill. Capture the first arrangement chosen in Round 1 before the constraint reveal, every commitment and reversal, and the child's stated reason. Ask nothing that repeats 6×4=24, 5×6=30, or an answer of 7. Keep total runtime near two minutes of real decisions; no praise animation longer than the decision it follows. Assignment: Festival Team Challenge. Academic construct to preserve: Lab Learner transfers equal-groups, missing-factor, and word-problem multiplication to unseen contexts at ceiling accuracy when the number pairs resemble rehearsed ones; the open question is whether that reasoning survives when a total admits several valid factor pairs and only a stated physical constraint selects the right one, and whether he re-plans rather than re-runs when the constraint changes mid-task. This node must test unseen synthesis: combine the construct in a context not practised before. Author fresh items. These have already been seen and must not be reused verbatim: math.multiplication.equal_groups, math.multiplication.missing_factor, math.multiplication.word_problem_interpretation, unseen.math.multiplication.equal_groups, unseen.math.multiplication.missing_factor, unseen.math.multiplication.word_problem_interpretation. Planner decision behind this node: Prediction was accuracy 0.70–1.00 on fresh unassisted transfer items; the Quest 'The Lantern Ferry Won't Wait' returned 3/3 correct, unassisted, exposure=unseen, provenance=independent_probe, with no confounds — accuracy 1.00, inside the preregistered band. Predicted factor-pair overgeneralization did not appear. But the theory's sharpest clause is still untested: no Quest item presented a total with multiple valid factor pairs where only a situational constraint selects the right one, and all three responses (6×4=24, 7, 5×6=30) mirror the rehearsed baseline answers exactly, so identical surface numbers remain a live alternative explanation. Transfer is supported at the evidence limit of independent performance; synthesis under constraint is unproven, which is exactly what a Boss must probe. Created at: 2026-08-08T18:10:46.085Z.",
  "evidenceContract": {
    "academic": true,
    "engagement": true,
    "companionObservations": true
  }
}

